@namespace SpaceSails.Client.Pages
@implements IHandleEvent
@using SurfaceExcursion = SpaceSails.Client.Pages.Map.SurfaceExcursion
@using System.Globalization
@using SpaceSails.Client.Rendering
@using SpaceSails.Contracts
@using SpaceSails.Core
@using SpaceSails.Core.Interior
@*
    #251 item 1 · THE LIFT PANEL, ON ITS OWN FILE.

    Moved out of Map.razor by PURE MOTION — the block between the two fences below is the block that was in
    the page, character for character. Every page member it reads is a [Parameter] with the SAME NAME (the
    fields keep their underscore, the methods come across as delegates), which is what lets the markup move
    without a rewrite and lets the suite's source guards read it through MapMarkup exactly as before.

    The `@if` that opens it stays in Map.razor: the page keeps its paint order in one place, and a closed
    surface still renders nothing. IHandleEvent repeats the page's own override (Map.Sim.cs) — without it
    Blazor would put an automatic re-render back on every event in here, which the page turns off because
    the HUD's refresh belongs to OnTick's 200 ms throttle. The .razor.css beside this file carries no rules;
    it exists to give the component Map.razor.css's scope (#251 item 3 moves the rules).

    The `@{ }` around the fences is not decoration: the block used to sit inside `@if (…) { … }`,
    which is a Razor CODE block, and a code block is where its first statements are legal. The braces
    put the moved lines back in the context they were written in; they are outside the fences, so what
    the source guards read is still exactly the lines that were in the page.
*@
@* ── THE PAGE'S OWN NOTE ON THIS SURFACE, moved here with it (#251 item 1) and not touched by a
   word: it is the paragraph that stood above this block in Map.razor. It sits ABOVE the fence, not
   inside it, so that a source guard slicing the block by text goes on reading markup and not prose.
   ── *@
    @* View-object popup: a Gen-AI image (a souvenir T-shirt, a fridge magnet, any lore prop) the
       player walks up to and inspects with E. Click away, hit Close, or press E again to dismiss. *@
    @* #600 · THE LIFT PANEL. Owner, having ridden a car that only ever went down: "we should have elevator
       panel with UI then (I wonder what the elevator music would be like ) :-D"

       The buttons come from Core (UndergroundComplex.LiftPanel) rather than being worked out here, so the
       #590 card gate and the #592 silence are one tested rule and this file only draws it. SURFACE is always
       the first button — that is the whole bug fix.

       Each button carries the one fact that decides whether the trip is free: whether that floor still holds
       air. A refusing button is PRESENT and says why; an absent button and a broken one look identical from
       the captain's side, and this ground has already shipped that mistake once. *@

@{
@* ── MARKUP BEGINS · verbatim from Map.razor ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── *@
        <div class="view-object-backdrop" @onclick="() => Dismiss(CloseLiftPanel)">
            <OverlayShell class="lift-panel" StopClicks="true"
                          Frame="OverlayFrame.Bare" Dismiss="OverlayDismiss.Close"
                          DismissClass="view-object-close" DismissFace="Close"
                          DismissTitle="Step out of the car"
                          OnClose="() => Dismiss(CloseLiftPanel)">
                <div class="view-object-title">🛗 CAR PANEL</div>
                @* #801 · WHICH car. The line is the panel's own no longer: two cars serve this band and
                   only one of them climbs out, so the sentence under the title is decided beside the stops
                   it is describing. *@
                <div class="lift-sub">
                    @LiftPanelLine() You are at
                    <strong>@SpaceSails.Core.UndergroundComplex.DepthPaint(liftEx.Floor)</strong>.
                </div>

                @* #686 · A refusing button answers HERE, inside the panel's own layer — the pulse HUD sits
                   under the backdrop blur, where the refusal is in the DOM and not on the screen. *@
                @if (_liftOutcome is { } refusedLine)
                {
                    <div class="lift-outcome">@refusedLine</div>
                }

                @* #719 slice 2 · THE MAINTENANCE BREAK, AND IT IS THE WHOLE OF WHAT THE FEATURE SAYS.
                   Somebody the captain has just been read by said the floor into a radio, and the directory
                   is gone: one plate where thirteen buttons were, in the stencil idiom every other sign
                   down here is painted in, and nothing anywhere that names who called it.

                   It REPLACES the list rather than sitting over it. A plate above a row of dead buttons
                   would be an instrument saying two things — here are your floors, and there are no floors
                   — and the way out of this building is now a flight of steps at the other end of the
                   corridor, which a captain reading a list of buttons is not looking for. *@
                @if (TheCarIsStopped)
                {
                    <div class="lift-stopped">@SpaceSails.Core.UndergroundComplex.CarStoppedPlate</div>
                }
                else
                {
                <div class="lift-stops">
                    @foreach (SpaceSails.Core.UndergroundComplex.LiftStop stop in LiftStops())
                    {
                        string cls = "lift-stop"
                            + (stop.IsCurrent ? " here" : "")
                            + (stop.Refusal is not null ? " sealed" : "")
                            + (stop.OpenedBy is not null ? " carded" : "")
                            + (stop.Pressurised ? "" : " dead");
                        <button type="button" class="@cls" disabled="@stop.IsCurrent"
                                title="@(stop.Refusal ?? (stop.Pressurised ? "holds pressure" : "dead air — the tank runs"))"
                                @onclick="() => PressLiftButton(stop)">
                            <span class="lift-stop-depth">@SpaceSails.Core.UndergroundComplex.DepthPaint(stop.Level)</span>
                            <span class="lift-stop-name">@stop.Name</span>
                            <span class="lift-stop-air">
                                @if (stop.IsCurrent)
                                {
                                    <text>◄ you are here</text>
                                }
                                else if (stop.OpenedBy is not null)
                                {
                                    @* #689 · The positive twin of "🔒 sealed" — the row a captain who went
                                       and FOUND the thing has earned. Core decides whether it is true. *@
                                    <text>🎫 opens for you</text>
                                }
                                else if (stop.Refusal is not null)
                                {
                                    <text>🔒 sealed</text>
                                }
                                else if (stop.Pressurised)
                                {
                                    <text>🫁 air</text>
                                }
                                else if (stop.HasRefuge)
                                {
                                    @* #608 · The one thing about the air a captain can learn BEFORE the
                                       doors open. The plan carries a pressure refuge on this floor; whether
                                       it still holds is not on the plan and this row never claims it is.
                                       What is beside it — the department's own plate — is the honest hint,
                                       because the floors that kept a maintenance line kept their air. *@
                                    <text>dead air · @SpaceSails.Core.UndergroundComplex.RefugeRowTag</text>
                                }
                                else
                                {
                                    <text>dead air</text>
                                }
                            </span>
                            @* #689 · …and WHICH paper does it, in the office's own register, so the card in
                               the satchel and the promise on the panel are the same words. Owner: "there was
                               no story point about it being needed or used." *@
                            @if (stop.OpenedBy is { } carriedCard)
                            {
                                <span class="lift-stop-card">@carriedCard — the gate will read it</span>
                            }
                        </button>
                        @* #602 · THE PAD, AND THE STICKER BESIDE IT. Owner, 2026-08-02, overruling #590's
                           call 3 deliberately: "a vicious security sticked warning next to it and it gives
                           you 3 tries before calling security".

                           THE STICKER IS DRAWN BEFORE THE KEYS, ALWAYS, and never behind a press. That is
                           the whole reason a keypad is allowed on this ground: the affordance states its own
                           cost, so a guess is not problem-solving, it is gambling with a price you were
                           told. A pad whose warning appeared after the first miss would be the punishment
                           without the bargain.

                           Which row carries one is Core's (LiftStop.HasPad) — one row, on the SEALED gate
                           into the first band, and never the ID CHECK. This file only draws it. *@
                        @if (stop.HasPad)
                        {
                            SpaceSails.Core.UndergroundComplex.LiftStop padStop = stop;
                            <div class="lift-pad">
                                <div class="lift-pad-sticker">⚠ @SpaceSails.Core.UndergroundComplex.LiftCode.Sticker</div>
                                <div class="pin-pad-display">@LiftPadDisplay</div>
                                <div class="pin-pad-keys">
                                    @foreach (string d in new[] { "1", "2", "3", "4", "5", "6", "7", "8", "9" })
                                    {
                                        string digit = d;
                                        <button type="button" class="pin-pad-key" disabled="@LiftPadIsDark"
                                                title="Key this digit into the lift code"
                                                @onclick="() => LiftPadPush(digit)">@digit</button>
                                    }
                                    <button type="button" class="pin-pad-key pin-pad-key-fn" disabled="@LiftPadIsDark"
                                            title="Clear the code and start over" @onclick="LiftPadClear">C</button>
                                    <button type="button" class="pin-pad-key" disabled="@LiftPadIsDark"
                                            title="Key a zero into the lift code" @onclick='() => LiftPadPush("0")'>0</button>
                                    <button type="button" class="pin-pad-key pin-pad-key-go" disabled="@LiftPadIsDark"
                                            title="Try this code on the gate"
                                            @onclick="() => LiftPadSubmit(padStop)">↵</button>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        }
                    }
                </div>
                }

                @* #602 · What the pad answered, in its own words and nobody else's — one of Core's four
                   plates. It is OUTSIDE the loop because the pad comes off the row the instant a code
                   works, and OPEN is exactly the answer a captain most needs to still be able to read. *@
                @if (LiftPadSaid is { } padSaid)
                {
                    <div class="lift-pad-said">@padSaid</div>
                }
            </OverlayShell>
        </div>
@* ── MARKUP ENDS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── *@
}

@code {
    // ── WHAT THESE PARAMETERS ARE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    //
    // #251 item 1 · Every [Parameter] below carries a member of the page under THE MEMBER'S OWN NAME —
    // a field keeps its underscore, a method arrives as a delegate with its own signature, a variable the
    // page's `@if` binds arrives under the name the `@if` gave it. That is the whole trick of this
    // refactor: it is what let the markup above move out of Map.razor without a single character of it
    // changing, and what lets the suite's source guards read it through MapMarkup exactly as they read it
    // when it lived in the page. A parameter that needs saying more than that says it on its own line.

    [Parameter] public string? _liftOutcome { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public Action CloseLiftPanel { get; set; } = default!;
    [Parameter] public Func<Action, Task> Dismiss { get; set; } = default!;
    [Parameter] public Action LiftPadClear { get; set; } = default!;
    [Parameter] public string LiftPadDisplay { get; set; } = default!;
    [Parameter] public bool LiftPadIsDark { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public Action<string> LiftPadPush { get; set; } = default!;
    [Parameter] public string? LiftPadSaid { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public Action<UndergroundComplex.LiftStop> LiftPadSubmit { get; set; } = default!;
    [Parameter] public Func<string> LiftPanelLine { get; set; } = default!;
    [Parameter] public Func<IReadOnlyList<UndergroundComplex.LiftStop>> LiftStops { get; set; } = default!;

    /// <summary>#719 slice 2 · Whether the car has been stopped under this captain. The page's own question
    /// (Map.Surface.Break.cs), asked here rather than inferred from an empty <see cref="LiftStops"/> — a
    /// panel that read "no rows" as "stopped" would paint the plate over any future silence, and the panel
    /// is not the place that knows what a silence means.</summary>
    [Parameter] public bool TheCarIsStopped { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public Action<UndergroundComplex.LiftStop> PressLiftButton { get; set; } = default!;
    [Parameter] public SurfaceExcursion liftEx { get; set; } = default!;

    // The page's own event dispatch, repeated: no automatic re-render per event.
    Task IHandleEvent.HandleEventAsync(EventCallbackWorkItem callback, object? arg) => callback.InvokeAsync(arg);
}
